A||||5||Getaway2k||Radio question||||5||
Z||000000||Getaway2k||08-03-2000||08:28 PM||||A few of you have mentioned that you replaced your Fiero radio with a newer CD unit from a Grand Am or Grand Prix (can't remember specifically which one). Did you buy these from a junk yard or are they new stock from the dealer? Also, if a junk yard purchase, how do you get around the security code issue (or is this code also provided at the bone-yard)? I'm looking to replace my cassette deck and would like to go the same route that some of you did. Any details that you could provide would be appreciated.<P>------------------<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://pages.prodigy.net/nuppy/getaway.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Raydar||08-03-2000||08:38 PM||||Try this.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.newellelectronics.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.newellelectronics.com/</A> <P>I usta tell folks about AATechnologies, but the guy (Jim?) asked me if I wanted to wait so he could find me a "new" radio instead of a refurbed one. Said he was getting "skids full" of radios. Told me to call him back in a couple of days. Called back about 4-5 times, over the period of 2 1/2 weeks. He was always "in a conference". Screw 'em.<BR> <BR>If you get a newer radio, you'll probably need a wiring harness adapter. If these folks don't have 'em, GM does. They also have info about the theft-lock codes.  <P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR>88 Formula T-Top <BR>88 Coupe (ISO big V-6)<P>"Some mornings, it just isn't worth<BR>chewing through the restraints."<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 08-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||tgowens||08-03-2000||09:46 PM||||hey Getaway, Raydar is right -- call Bobby newell -- he also has the radio/antenna adapter you need - he charges $15 for the and they list for $18.25 from a dealer (although I was quoted as high as $42.50 by one dealer) -- Bobby can also step you through how to get the backup code needed - once you have the backup code he can get the unlock code for you. You can try the junk yards but honestly you will pay darn near as much from a junk yard as you would if you bought one from Bobby in the first place. Anyway, give him a call (he has an 800# in Beaumont) and tell him I said hello!! (I haven't talked to him for a while -- he helped me with mine and didn't charge me a dime!!!)<P>Here is mine...... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/my_interior_3.jpg"> ......it is out of a '98 Grand Am and with the equalizer it sounds so good I can't turn the factory sub up all the way!!! (broke the sound barrier last time I did that)<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/MY_SIGNATURE.gif">               <BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Getaway2k||08-03-2000||10:23 PM||||Thanks to the both of you for the info.<P>Terry-your set-up is exactly what I'm looking for. I have the 800# from the link that Raydar provided. I'm going to give this guy a call in the morning.<P>Thanks again.<P>------------------<BR>         <IMG SRC="http://pages.prodigy.net/nuppy/getaway.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Getaway2k (edited 08-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||BN Boomer||08-04-2000||12:22 AM||||I have pretty much the same setup as Terry (tgowens). This one came from a 97 Pontiac Sunfire. I'm really happy with this radio. All I need to do now is upgrade the speakers and trying to figure out how to add some sort of small subwoofer.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/boomers_fiero_files/85gt1.jpg"> <P>I'll second, third, or whatever the recommendation for Newell Electronics. They sell the adapter for $18.20 <I>shipped</I>, that's less than you'll find it at any dealer, assuming you can find a local dealer with it at all (I couldn't). Not only did they have a great price on the adapter, but they were incredibly helpful in helping me gain the THEFTLOCK code to operate my radio.<P>If Bobby can't source one of these radios for you, they can usually be found at <A HREF="http://www.ebay.com" TARGET=_blank>Ebay</A>, albeit also for usually a hefty price. <P>Another source would be to go to <A HREF="http://www.car-part.com" TARGET=_blank>Car-Part.com</A> and search for radios from 96-99 Sunfires and Grand Ams. I understand Pontiac gone to a double DIN deck for 2000, so the current radios won't fit in a Fiero. It looks like a decent AM/FM/CD radio can be found from about $150 on up.<P>I'll further go ahead and let the cat out of the bag on where I found the two I bought, one for $50 and the other $60. Keep an eye on the audio forum at <A HREF="http://www.j-body.org" TARGET=_blank>J-Body.org</A>. There are quite a few Sunfire owners there who have upgraded their stereo systems and some of them are willing to let the stock units go pretty cheap. I imagine you could also search for other Pontiac, Sunfire, or Grand Am websites and ask if anyone has a factory CD player they are interested in selling. Good luck. ||||reg||14
Z||000005||Indiana_resto_guy||08-04-2000||09:15 AM||||You can also get the antenna adapter from the large style found in Fieros to the late model size form NAPA part #730-1183 4 $11.00.<BR>Not sure but I think that I got the plug adapter from a local Radio Shack but can't remember how much it was.||||reg||1
